          "primary_locator": "https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/v257/src/core/timer.c#L393-L431",
          "sha256": "675b20653abc17d52632ce48e6c2cd1ebcbebcf72394f2bf5909180ee859ce12"
        }
      ],
      "finding": "The required prospective check asserts a journal-observed service start at boot within AccuracySec. Persistent catch-up is conditional at timer activation, and AccuracySec governs timer elapse coalescing, subject to timer slack; it is not a service-execution or boot-to-start upper bound. Calendar timer activation has default ordering after sysinit/time-set/time-sync. The implementation submits JOB_START after elapse; service pre-start work can further precede ExecStart. Thus a timer activated after an ordering delay longer than one minute can correctly catch up yet fail this check. Even interpreting at boot broadly as during startup does not make AccuracySec a service-start deadline.",
      "consequence": "A required acceptance oracle can reject correct systemd-257 behavior and cannot establish the claimed timing property. The body contains the relevant caveats, but the finish-line check drops them.",
